Context
A parent needs a parental control system for a 10-year-old running Edubuntu. The system must enforce daily screen time limits, block age-inappropriate websites, and provide AI-powered activity reports — all manageable via a local web dashboard. The AI layer (Claude claude-sonnet-4-6) adds smart content classification beyond static blocklists and generates natural-language behavior summaries for the parent.

Architecture Overview
Child's Firefox → dnsmasq (port 53) → static blocklist OR AI classifier cache
                                    → unknown domain queue → Claude API (nightly batch)

EduGuard Daemon (root, systemd)
  ├── time_tracker    polls psutil every 60s, writes to SQLite
  ├── enforcer        calls loginctl lock-session when limits hit
  ├── browser_monitor copies + reads Firefox places.sqlite every 5 min
  └── scheduler       APScheduler (max_instances=1 on all jobs)

Flask Dashboard (127.0.0.1:5000, parent only, Flask-WTF CSRF)
  ├── reads SQLite for reports/insights
  └── writes eduguard.yaml + sends SIGUSR1 to daemon for hot-reload

SQLite (/var/lib/eduguard/eduguard.db) — WAL mode enabled at connection time
  └── tables: usage_sessions, browser_visits, blocked_attempts,
              ai_decisions (cache), app_usage, ai_insights,
              config_audit, job_runs, browser_history_cursor

